<!--
TEMPLATE: substitute the tokens listed below. One rendered file per resource, living in
company/resources/ (consumer-owned). The REGISTRY.md index links to each.

POINTERS ONLY — a resource entry NEVER contains credential material (no passwords, tokens,
keys, cookies, session data). It records that a capability EXISTS, how an agent reaches it,
and who owns the grant. The registry is redaction-SENSITIVE: its names/vendors/hostnames
feed propose-to-core's blocklist and must never appear in outbound artifacts.

Substitution tokens:
  <<RESOURCE_NAME>>   — kebab-case identifier (e.g. cloudflare-dns, founder-chrome, company-email)
  <<KIND>>            — cli | mcp | browser-cdp | account | api
  <<OWNER_HUMAN>>     — the human who granted/owns this access (single-operator today; named per Risk 36)
  <<STEWARD_AGENT>>   — agent responsible for the entry's accuracy (default: people-lead)
  <<CREATED_DATE>>    — YYYY-MM-DD

Kind-specific guidance:
  cli          — an authenticated CLI on some machine (gh, wrangler, aws). `access:` = the command;
                 `machines:` lists where it is authed.
  mcp          — a registered MCP server. `access:` = the server name in .mcp.json (registering it
                 is acquire-resource's register-mcp-server step — a human-gated settings change).
  browser-cdp  — the operator's real browser driven via CDP, carrying their logged-in sessions.
                 THE RULES: non-headless only (actions visible to the operator); per-machine
                 (`machines:` required); TRY IT FIRST for any task an account can do (DNS, domains,
                 email, SaaS admin) and escalate to the human only after a documented failed attempt;
                 payment/checkout/billing actions are NEVER taken this way without explicit human
                 go-ahead per action (never-automate invariant 5); no credential exfiltration —
                 the browser is used, never harvested.
  account      — a service account/login that exists but has no automated access path yet;
                 `access:` describes the human process to use it.
  api          — a direct API with auth from env/MCP; `access:` = base URL + auth source pointer
                 (e.g. "env: CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN"), never the token itself.
-->

---
resource: <<RESOURCE_NAME>>
kind: <<KIND>>
owner_human: <<OWNER_HUMAN>>
steward_agent: <<STEWARD_AGENT>>
created: <<CREATED_DATE>>
machines: []            # kinds cli/browser-cdp: which machines have this live (per-machine, like scheduled-processes.json)
allowed_agents: []      # empty = any agent may use it within the kind rules; else an allow-list
status: active          # active | revoked | pending-grant
---

# <<RESOURCE_NAME>>

## What it gives the company

One paragraph: which task classes this resource unlocks (e.g. "DNS + domain management for all company domains via the operator's Cloudflare account").

## Access

How an agent actually uses it, per the kind guidance above. Pointers only.

## Escalation

Who to hand off to when using it fails (default: `owner_human`), and what a hand-off must include per the tried-and-failed contract (what was attempted, exact failure, the smallest unblocking action requested).
